 a very long time  so kind of after that's done  you kind of cook it normally as you  would  [Music]  and the trickiest part is transferring  that slab of beautiful glazed meat onto  a nice bun and the key here is to  actually get a nice piece of toasted  bread that matches whatever the uh ribs  you're gonna make so as long as you get  some bread and you toast it and you  butter it and it fits with the rib  you know you can add whatever toppings  you want i like onions so i chose to add  onions and some parsley  [Music]  the coolest part is when you actually  serve it because when you slice it along  the edges of the sandwich you're  actually going to get one rib per person  and it's cause like that's like one  sandwich per person  so you just cut it up into what you  think where the ribs were everybody gets  a nice sandwich that they can  just chomp on and it's you know it's  like a boneless rib and what could be  better than that i was surprised it  actually turned out really well it's  kind of like your childhood uh you know  fast food rib sandwich reimagined with  actual ribs because who knows what's in  the original one and if you know you do  make it tag me send it over i think it's  awesome when you guys make our recipes  so i think that's a keeper for sure if  you want to entertain if you just want  some ribs or if you're just looking for  lunch like me on a weekly basis  [Music]  during my time at tasty i've gone  through a lot of phases there was you  know the giant food phase and at one  point the recurring phase was the layer  phase i  absolutely love layers i think they're  beautiful i think that can be really  cool very visual very stunning and can  incorporate a lot of flavor so i was  definitely in a phase of just making  foods that had beautiful layers to them  i love cheesecake a lot of people love  cheesecake i love cookies and cream a  lot of people love cookies and cream so  i had this idea of making a cookies and  cream cheesecake that was just purely  black and white but had the cleanest  layers possible  the thought process was that i would  take you know the base would be the  cookies the filling would be the  cheesecake that also uses the fillings  from the cookies and the top would be a  thin layer of like those like pulverized  like ground or fine powdered of the  cookies so you get like a black white  black look so pretty much is you start  with the base you make the filling and  then you do the top so the base is just  crushed pulverized cookies that you mix  with some butter  [Music]  you bake like a graham cracker crust  it's pretty simple you just put it in  and bake it you kind of really want to  press it flat so there's no huge lumps  so that goes in and after you separate  the filling from the cookies you want to  save the cookie filling to make sure  that you can use that for the cheesecake  filling mix out with some cream cheese  and it's a pretty standard piece of  batter recipe what makes this a no-bake  recipe is that it uses gelatin to help  set the mixture so gelatin mixture is  then whisked back in to the cheesecake  mixture so it creates this really smooth  really nice really velvety kind of  texture that also has the melted cookie  filling in it so you don't really waste  anything here it's kind of you know you  separate the cookie into the crust you  separate the filling into the cheesecake  and it's basically redefining the shape  of what the cookie looked like in the  first plate once that filling is set you  want to pour that into the  baked cheesecake mold and that kind of  sets in the fridge you want to make sure  that it's as smooth as possible no lumps  if you really need to and you see lumps  and flecks of stuff  you can sieve it you can patch it  through a mesh sieve to get rid of any  impurities if you really want to that  goes in the fridge for a very long time  if you actually want the cleanest layers  possible you can throw it in the freezer  it's not going to make a 